WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.840
Removable media has long been a malware delivery path because it crosses boundaries between systems, networks, and trust zones.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.780
Modern platforms have reduced some older autorun behaviors, but risk remains.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:22.960
A drive may contain suspicious executables, scripts, shortcut files, archives, document macros, installers, or files designed to entice a user into unsafe handling.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:30.220
Autorun history matters at a high level because older environments and legacy configurations may have behaved differently than modern locked-down systems.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:35.160
Investigators should understand that automatic execution is not a universal assumption.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:41.860
A file being present on a USB device does not prove it executed, and a device connection does not prove malware ran.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.920
Execution claims require supporting host or telemetry evidence.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.940
Safe triage means not opening or running unknown files on production systems.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:01:01.880
Suspicious files should be handled through approved processes, isolated analysis environments, static review, controlled detonation where authorized, or specialized malware analysis workflows.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:09.620
The details depend on organizational policy and expertise, but the principle is simple: do not turn evidence review into an infection event.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:21.360
Shortcut files, scripts, archives, and macro-enabled documents deserve careful review because they can contain references, commands, paths, embedded content, or execution clues.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.980
At the same time, risk documentation should avoid overclaiming intent.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:33.560
A suspicious file may indicate risk, staging, prior infection, testing material, or unrelated storage.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.700
The conclusion should match the evidence.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:44.400
A good risk review documents what was observed, where it was located, how it was handled, and what additional analysis would be required to determine behavior.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:52.140
It separates file presence from execution, suspicious characteristics from confirmed maliciousness, and technical capability from user intent.
